</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(ii) Anchor and expression rules.</span>  
The anchor rules are central to the SSA-based design and are what ground
LLM-generated constraints to precise program locations.
For <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AFTER_DEF</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BEFORE_</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">USE</span> constraints, the prompt
mandates that <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">anchor.var</span> be the SSA-versioned name (<em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"var": "x__2"</span>), not the original variable name.
This requirement eliminates ambiguity in programs where the same variable is
assigned multiple times along different control-flow paths.
For <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LOOP_HEAD</span>/<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LOOP_TAIL</span> constraints, the prompt mandates
the integer loop identifier from the <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"># loop__id: N</span> annotation
in the SSA form.
Expressions in <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">spec.expr</span> must also use SSA-versioned names for
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AFTER_DEF</span>/<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BEFORE_USE</span> contexts, while
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ENTRY</span> constraints use the original parameter names
since those regions are not redefined.
The expression language is restricted to a safe subset of Python:
comparisons, arithmetic, and built-in aggregators
(<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">len</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">any</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sum</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abs</span>),
ensuring that constraint expressions are side-effect-free and
executable at runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(iii) Program source and SSA form.</span>  
The prompt includes both the original function source
and its SSA-transformed version with the definition map rendered as
inline comments (e.g., <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shifted__1 -&gt; ’shifted’ (byte 83)</span>).
The SSA form provides the LLM with explicit variable versioning,
so that each definition–use chain is unambiguous.
The definition map further tells the LLM, and <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span>’s grounding
pass, exactly where each SSA variable is defined in the source,
enabling byte-precise instrumentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(iv) Passing and failing tests.</span>  
The prompt includes concrete passing test cases with expected outputs
and failing test cases with error tracebacks.
This test-outcome context guides the LLM toward constraints that
capture the semantic difference between passing and failing executions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">LLM invocation and grounding.</span>  
<span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> queries the LLM in zero-shot mode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al. (2023)</a></cite>, instructing the model to return only valid JSON conforming to
the <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cbfl-ir</span> schema.
Returned constraints are validated against the schema; malformed constraints
are discarded.
Each retained constraint is then <em id="S3.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grounded</em>: <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> looks up the
definition map for the SSA name in the constraint anchor, resolving it to a
precise byte-level insertion site.
Constraints referencing non-existent SSA names or loop identifiers are
discarded as ungroundable; for anchors that match multiple sites (<em id="S3.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> the same
SSA variable used in both branches of a conditional), one instrumented check
is generated per site.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Semantic Spectrum Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Instrumentation.</span>  
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> implements one instrumentation handler per region type that locates the
correct byte range, computes indentation, and inserts a
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">__cbfl.check(cid, lambda: <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> call.
All edits are collected as non-overlapping byte-range replacements applied in
reverse source order.
Temporal constraints additionally maintain per-test state using Python
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ContextVar</span>s for correct isolation across parallel test
invocations.
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> integrates with <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pytest</span> via a plugin that initializes a
per-test context at setup and writes constraint violations to JSONL records
after each test.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Semantic Spectrum Matrix.</span>  
After execution, <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> constructs the semantic spectrum matrix
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\{0,1\}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\{0,1\}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> iff constraint <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
is violated during test <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each constraint <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{f}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{f}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (violated in failing tests),
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (violated in passing tests), <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{f}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{f}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (satisfied in failing tests),
and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (satisfied in passing tests)—mirroring the coverage cells of
traditional SBFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones et al. (2002)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Souza et al. (2017)</a></cite> but computed over semantic
constraints.
<span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> scores each constraint using the Ochiai coefficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abreu et al. (2009b)</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(C_{j})\;=\;\frac{e^{f}_{j}}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{T}^{-}|\cdot(e^{f}_{j}+e^{p}_{j})}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(C_{j})\;=\;\frac{e^{f}_{j}}{\sqrt{|\mathcal{T}^{-}|\cdot(e^{f}_{j}+e^{p}_{j})}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Each violated constraint propagates its score to the statement that
<em id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">establishes</em> the violated property: the defining assignment for
<span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AFTER_DEF</span> constraints, the call site for <span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRECONDITION</span>
constraints, and the statements in the backward slice from the return value
for <span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">POSTCONDITION</span> constraints.
When multiple constraints attribute to the same statement <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the statement
score is taken as the maximum:
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma(s)=\max_{C_{j}:\,s\in\mathit{attr}(C_{j})}\sigma(C_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑎𝑡𝑡𝑟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(s)=\max_{C_{j}:\,s\in\mathit{attr}(C_{j})}\sigma(C_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Counterfactual Verification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic spectrum analysis can produce two classes of noise: <em id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">over-approximate
constraints</em> violated in both passing and failing tests, and <em id="S3.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cascading
violations</em> where an upstream fault propagates to inflate downstream constraint
scores.
<span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> addresses both through counterfactual verification, formalized in
Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.3 Agentic Constraint Inference ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SemLoc: Structured Grounding of Free-Form LLM Reasoningfor Fault Localization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Over-approximation detection.</span>  
Before any LLM call, <span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> inspects the violations log produced during semantic
spectrum execution and identifies <em id="S3.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">over-approximate</em> constraints, those
that fired on at least one passing test.
This detection is purely factual: any constraint that flags correct behavior
as invalid cannot serve as a discriminative fault signal and is immediately
classified as <span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OverApproximate</span> and skipped.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Counterfactual patching.</span>  
For each remaining constraint <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with a non-zero Ochiai score, an LLM
generates a minimal, syntactically local <em id="S3.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">counterfactual patch</em> <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
that would restore the violated property at the attributed source line
(temperature 0 for determinism).
<span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> replaces exactly that source line with <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (preserving indentation),
writes the patched program to a temporary directory mirroring the original
package structure, and re-executes <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> via a fresh <span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pytest</span>
invocation with an adjusted <span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PYTHONPATH</span>.
The outcome determines the causal status of <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Primary</span>: <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> causes all <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> tests to pass;
the attributed statement is the root-cause fault location.
Verification terminates early.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secondary</span>: <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces but does not eliminate failures;
<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> captures a cascading or partial fault signal.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Irrelevant</span>: <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> has no effect on test outcomes;
<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is not discriminative and is pruned.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Redundancy pruning.</span>  
After the main loop, <span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> applies dominance based pruning: constraint <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="S3.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dominated</em> by a higher-ranked constraint <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (Primary or Secondary) if
the set of tests still failing after applying <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of those
still failing after applying <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Dominated constraints are marked redundant and removed from the final ranking,
as they contribute no additional diagnostic information beyond what <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
already captures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The final ranking retains only statements attributed by primary constraints.
Counterfactual verification provides a <em id="S3.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sufficient</em> condition for
root-cause identification; when multi-location faults prevent any single-constraint
patch from resolving all failures, <span id="S3.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> falls back to the spectrum-based ranking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Working Example</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trace <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> on the softmax program from Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Motivating Example ‣ SemLoc: Structured Grounding of Free-Form LLM Reasoningfor Fault Localization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The SSA transformation renames the single assignment to <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">m</span> as
<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">m__1</span> and the single assignment to <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shifted</span> as
<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shifted__1</span>.
Using these SSA-versioned names in the prompt, the LLM produces four grounded
constraints:
<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRECONDITION</span>, <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ENTRY</span>): <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">len(xs) &gt; 0</span>;
<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RELATION</span>, <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AFTER_DEF</span> on <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">m__1</span>): <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">m__1 == max(xs)</span>;
<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VALUE_RANGE</span>, <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AFTER_DEF</span> on <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shifted__1</span>):
<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all(v &lt;= 0 for v in shifted__1)</span>;
<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">POSTCONDIT-</span> <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ION</span>, <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ANY_RETURN</span>):
<span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abs(sum(result)-1.0)&lt;1e-6 and all(0&lt;=v&lt;=1 for v in result)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After instrumented execution over <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are satisfied
in all tests (<math id="S3.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is violated in both passing and failing
tests, yielding a low Ochiai score. <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is violated only in the failing
tests <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(C_{4})=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(C_{4})=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.
During counterfactual verification, the patch for <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (adding a division
guard at Line 8) fails to resolve <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>—<math id="S3.SS6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is
classified as <em id="S3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">secondary</em>.
The patch for <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (correcting Line 5 from <span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">x + m</span> to
<span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">x - m</span>) causes all four tests to pass, Thus, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is
classified as <em id="S3.SS6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primary</em>.
The refined ranking assigns full suspiciousness to Line 5 only, exactly
matching the ground-truth fault location.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Implementation.</span>  
<span id="S3.SS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> is implemented in Python using
Tree-sitter v0.25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brunsfeld et al. (2026)</a></cite> for parsing AST-based SSA transformation and instrumentation.
Constraint inference and counterfactual patch generation both use
Gemini 2.5 Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google (2025)</a></cite>; the backend is
configurable and also supports Claude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2025)</a></cite> APIs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We answer three research questions:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
