<td id="S4.T3.16.1.23.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">56.1</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.23.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">64.5</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.23.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">6.9</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.23.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">3</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.16.1.24" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">AutoFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al. (2024)</a></cite></td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Gemini 2.5 Pro</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">—</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">81.5<sup id="S4.T3.16.1.24.4.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup></td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">—</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">—</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">—</td>
<td id="S4.T3.16.1.24.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">—</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.T3.17" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><sup id="S4.T3.17.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> AutoFL predicts the buggy <em id="S4.T3.17.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">function</em>, not the line; 81.5% is function-level Acc@1.</p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Baselines.</span>  
We compare <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> against following baselines:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Spectrum-based fault localization (SBFL)</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abreu et al. (2009b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones et al. (2002)</a></cite> ranks statements by the Ochiai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abreu et al. (2009b)</a></cite>
or Tarantula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones et al. (2002)</a></cite> coefficient computed from per-test line coverage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Delta Debugging</em> (DD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeller (1999)</a></cite> iteratively removes statements
while preserving the fail/pass split and reports the minimal retaining
set as suspicious.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">AutoFL</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al. (2024)</a></cite> is an LLM agent using Gemini 2.5 Pro that interactively
invokes code navigation tools to identify the buggy <em id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">function</em>;
it operates at function granularity and is evaluated with function level Acc@1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> <sup id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup> </em> disables semantic indexing: the LLM is queried
to predict the faulty line directly from source code and test failure
information, with no semantic indexing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><span id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> <sup id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">+</span></sup> </em> retains full constraint inference and semantic spectrum
scoring but removes the counterfactual verification step, isolating the
contribution of causal pruning in distinguishing primary violations from
overapproximate noise.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Metrics.</span>  
We report three fault localization metrics.
<span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc@k</span> is the fraction of programs where the ground truth faulty line
appears within the top-<math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> ranked lines.
<span id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean %Susp</span> is the mean fraction of executable lines flagged as
suspicious, which approximates developer inspection effort.
<span id="S4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Med. Rank</span> is the median rank of the faulty line in the suspiciousness
ordering.
We use <em id="S4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">worst-case tie-breaking</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Souza et al. (2017)</a></cite> throughout: when
multiple lines share an identical suspiciousness score, the faulty line is
ranked <em id="S4.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">last</em> among all tied lines, yielding a conservative lower bound
on Acc@<math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Implementation.</span>  
<span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> uses Tree-sitter version 0.25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brunsfeld et al. (2026)</a></cite> for AST parsing and
structural anchor extraction.
Constraints are inferred by Claude Sonnet 4.6 and emitted in <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cbfl-ir</span>.
All experiments run on a Linux workstation with Ubuntu 22.04 and 32 parallel workers.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ1: Localization Effectiveness</h3>

<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Semantic index region ablation.
<span id="S4.T4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coverage</span>: programs with at least one violated constraint in the region.
<span id="S4.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discrim. %</span>: fraction of violated constraints that are discriminative.
<math id="S4.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc@3/5</span>: performance drop when the region is removed.
<span id="S4.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wt.</span>: granularity weight in scoring.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T4.10" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:73.6pt;vertical-align:-35.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-112.3pt,24.0pt) scale(0.605719447761938,0.605719447761938) ;">
