<div id="S4.F4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:118pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29109v1/constraint.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163"></object>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Constraint distribution per program, sorted by number of violations.
Constraints are classified by violation counts: discriminative (<math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ef{&gt;}0,ep{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ef{&gt;}0,ep{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>), over-approximate (<math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ef{&gt;}0,ep{&gt;}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ef{&gt;}0,ep{&gt;}0</annotation></semantics></math>), and irrelevant (<math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ef{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ef{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>).
Most programs contain violated constraints, with over half being discriminative.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ2: Structured Semantic Oracles for Fault Localization</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> infers 1,023 semantic constraints across 250 programs, with 4.09 constraints per program on average.
In 233 programs (93.2%), at least one constraint is violated on failing tests, showing that the inferred constraints act as executable oracles that expose semantic inconsistencies at runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among the 383 violated constraints, 214 (55.9%) are discriminative, meaning they are violated only on failing tests.
This provides a reliable across-test signal that is absent in coverage-based methods.
Rather than relying on syntactic differences, <span id="S4.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> observes where semantic expectations fail, which directly guides localization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, 169 violated constraints (44.1%) are over-approximate and fire on both passing and failing tests.
This noise explains why semantic indexing alone is insufficient for precise ranking.
Counterfactual verification performs causal reasoning over these oracle signals and identifies which violations are responsible for the failure.
This converts raw semantic signals into causal evidence, improving Acc@1 from 30.8% to 42.8%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Structured anchoring guides localization.</span>  
Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ RQ1: Localization Effectiveness ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ SemLoc: Structured Grounding of Free-Form LLM Reasoningfor Fault Localization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that anchoring constraints to program regions determines how oracle signals are used. <span id="S4.SSx2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Line</span> anchors provide the strongest signal.
Removing them reduces Acc@3 by 25.2 percentage points and Acc@5 by 34.0 percentage points.
They turn the LLM prediction into an executable hypothesis and verify it across tests, enabling the system to confirm or reject the predicted location.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Other regions provide complementary signals.
Each non-<span id="S4.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Line</span> region contributes about 2.0 percentage points at Acc@3 and 4.0 percentage points at Acc@5.
Together, they lift Acc@3 from 66.0% to 68.0% and recover cases where the predicted line is incorrect.
These regions capture semantic inconsistencies at control flow and data flow boundaries, including branches, definitions, loops, and returns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the structured semantic index provides broad coverage and complementary signals.
It combines executable semantic oracles with structured anchoring to guide fault localization across diverse failure patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="S4.SSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S4.SSx2.p7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S4.SSx2.p7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SSx2.p7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Structured semantic grounding constructs executable oracles that guide fault localization.
These oracles provide across-test semantic signals that are unavailable to coverage-based methods.
Semantic indexing exposes where program behavior violates expected semantics, while counterfactual reasoning identifies which violations are causal.
Together, they convert LLM predictions into verifiable and actionable localization signals.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ3: Repository-Scale Portability</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess real world applicability, we apply <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> to <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BugsInPy</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Widyasari et al. (2020)</a></cite>, a curated collection of 463 confirmed bugs drawn from 17 actively maintained Python repositories including <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">youtube-dl</span>, <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">thefuck</span>, <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">black</span>, <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pandas</span>, <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fastapi</span>, and <span id="S4.SSx3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ansible</span>. These projects span CLI utilities, data science libraries, web frameworks, and developer tooling, representing the breadth of real world Python software.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Setup.</span>   We use AutoFL as the function level localization backbone: its interactive LLM agent explores the repository, invokes coverage and code snippet tools, and identifies the most suspicious function. <span id="S4.SSx3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> then applies its full constraint inference, instrumentation, violation collection, and spectrum scoring pipeline to produce a ranked list of suspicious <em id="S4.SSx3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lines</em> within that function. This two stage design mirrors the practical workflow that the paper targets. An LLM agent first performs coarse repository navigation, and <span id="S4.SSx3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> then turns that function level suspicion into precise line level evidence inside the function. Experiments use Claude Sonnet 4.6 at <math id="S4.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}0.8</annotation></semantics></math> for constraint inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Results.</span>  
Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ RQ3: Repository-Scale Portability ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ SemLoc: Structured Grounding of Free-Form LLM Reasoningfor Fault Localization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports line-level results on <span id="S4.SSx3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">youtube-dl</span>,
where the full pipeline completes on 7 confirmed BugsInPy bugs.
On this subset, <span id="S4.SSx3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> achieves 57.1% Acc@1, 85.7% Acc@3, and
100% Acc@5, while marking only 11.9% of executable lines as
suspicious.
Although this sample is small, it provides initial evidence that the method can
produce useful line-level rankings in real repositories once a target function is
available.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span><span id="S4.T6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span> on real-world <span id="S4.T6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BugsInPy</span> bugs (<span id="S4.T6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">youtube-dl</span>, <math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}7</annotation></semantics></math>),
built on top of AutoFL as the function-level backbone.
<span id="S4.T6.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank</span>: position of ground-truth faulty line in <span id="S4.T6.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemLoc</span>’s suspiciousness
ordering.
<span id="S4.T6.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">%Susp</span>: fraction of executable lines flagged suspicious (lower is better).
✓/×: whether the faulty line appears in the top-<math id="S4.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> ranked lines.
</figcaption>
